11. How to render the information to HTML container in reaxtg
    Step 1: Create an HTML container
    <div id="root"></div>
    This creates an empty container in your HTML page.

    Step 2: Select the container and render information
    const container = document.querySelector('#root');
    ReactDOM.createRoot(container).render(
      'Hello, Welcome to React!'
    );

    --document.querySelector('#root') selects the HTML element with the ID root.
    --ReactDOM.createRoot(container) creates a React root connected to that element.
    --.render('Hello, Welcome to React!') tells React to display the supplied text inside the container.

12. What is Babel?
    Babel is a JavaScript compiler that transforms modern JavaScript syntax and JSX into JavaScript that the target
    environment can understand.
    For example, React developers write JSX like this:
    const element = <h1>Hello React</h1>;
    Browsers do not directly understand JSX syntax. Babel can transform it into JavaScript calls that React can use to
    create the UI.

13. Why are you using Babel in your course?
    Your HTML contains:
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/supersimpledev/babel.js"></script>

    <script type="text/babel">
      const container = document.querySelector('.js-container');

      ReactDOM.createRoot(container).render(
        'Welcome to SuperSimpleDev React Course'
      );
    </script>
    --The Babel script loads the course's in-browser Babel transformer.
    --type="text/babel" tells that transformer to process the script.
    --Babel makes JSX usable by transforming it into JavaScript.
    --Your current render() example uses a string, not JSX, so Babel isn't needed for that particular expression. It's
      included to support JSX as you progress through the course.

14. what is JSX?
    JSX stands for JavaScript XML. It is a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows us to write HTML-like markup inside
    JavaScript code when building React user interfaces.
    Example without JSX:-
    const element = React.createElement(
      'h1',
      null,
      'Hello React!'
    );
    Here, we use React.createElement() to describe the UI element.

    Example with JSX
    const element = <h1>Hello React!</h1>;

